If a bit-source is non-junk at depth n and junk at depth n+1, its depth-n neighbourhood meets the branch selected by bit n in the empty set: the coded Y neighbourhood when the bit is true, and the complement of that neighbourhood in the master when the bit is false.

If the bit is true, the bit-source agrees through bit n with its positive twin, so the twin is junk, and the exclusion lemma gives disjointness from the coded Y sequence. If the bit is false, the negative refinement is empty. The atom is the union of its positive and negative refinements, so it equals the positive refinement. That atom is nonempty, so the positive check reads 0, and the positive twin is therefore non-junk with its nth bit set. The successor formula at the twin, together with the twin congruence, identifies the old code with the intersection of the old neighbourhood and the coded Y sequence, so the old neighbourhood is contained in the coded Y neighbourhood and misses its complement in the master.
